1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,699
Host: What is AgentWire?

2
00:00:01,699 --> 00:00:19,658
Expert: A change monitor for the things production agents depend on. Every morning it diffs the manifests, tool lists, schemas, changelogs and deprecation pages of forty-eight MCP servers, plus the OpenAI, Anthropic and Gemini APIs, and shows you what changed before it breaks you.

3
00:00:20,258 --> 00:00:25,144
Host: And where does WebMCP come in?

4
00:00:25,144 --> 00:00:42,540
Expert: The people who need this already run agents. So on load, the page registers four tools on document dot model context, with JSON schemas. Three read, one writes. Any agent in this browser can call them: no API key, no separate server, no scraping. And every call is logged on the page, where the person can see it.

5
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:46,152
Host: Let's ask the browser agent what changed this week.

6
00:00:46,152 --> 00:00:57,614
Expert: It calls list changes with severity notice. The call lands in the panel with its arguments and timing, and the change log filters itself. The person sees exactly what the agent saw.

7
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:16,899
Host: Is the Neon MCP server safe to update?

8
00:01:16,899 --> 00:01:20,126
Expert: That's check dependency. Neon fuzzy-matches the Neon MCP server, the source lights up, and the agent gets its last thirty days of changes, plus whether our last daily run actually fetched its URLs. So "no changes" never means "we couldn't check".

9
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:31,006
Host: Show me the diff.

10
00:01:31,006 --> 00:01:33,486
Expert: Get diff returns the stored lines for that one change. The row opens on screen at the same moment the agent reads it. Same data, same page, both of you.

11
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:44,547
Host: Now subscribe me to those two.

12
00:01:44,547 --> 00:01:57,454
Expert: This is the one write. The tool is annotated as not read-only, and its description tells the agent to confirm with you first. When it calls watch dependencies, the form fills in and confirms exactly what was saved.

13
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:02,516
Host: Can a person run the same thing?

14
00:02:02,516 --> 00:02:13,854
Expert: One click. The sample button calls the same function, and it shows up in the same log, tagged human, right next to the agent's calls. One code path for both.

15
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:33,487
Host: How do you know this is real?

16
00:02:33,487 --> 00:02:35,398
Expert: This is the production site, in Chrome one fifty-two with WebMCP enabled. The browser invokes each tool through the DevTools WebMCP channel, the same path an agent uses, and a dependency-free script in the repo reproduces it. The recorded run is committed.

17
00:02:36,798 --> 00:02:43,831
Expert: AgentWire. Dependency watch for production agents, with the agent on the page. Open source, MIT, on GitHub. Thanks.

